# FOODIIE

A food content site: a PHP admin CMS backed by SQLite, with a static site
generator that publishes plain HTML into `public/`. The public site is fully
static (fast, cheap to host); the CMS stays on a PHP host.

```
┌──────────┐   writes to    ┌──────────┐   generates    ┌──────────┐
│  Admin   │ ─────────────▶ │  SQLite  │ ─────────────▶ │ public/  │
│  (PHP)   │   (articles,   │   CMS    │   (static HTML │ (static  │
│          │   recipes,      │  data    │    + assets)   │  site)   │
│          │   videos…)     │          │                │          │
└──────────┘                └──────────┘                └──────────┘
        cms/generator/generate.php reads the DB and writes public/
```

## Run on WAMP (Windows) — quick start

> **PHP version:** FOODIIE runs on **PHP 7.4 or newer** — your WAMP's
> PHP 7.4.9 works fine. PHP 8.x is recommended for production/live
> servers, but not required.

1. Unzip the project to `C:\wamp64\www\foodiie` (so you have
   `C:\wamp64\www\foodiie\install\index.php`).
2. Enable SQLite extensions: click the **WAMP tray icon → PHP → PHP
   extensions → tick `php_pdo_sqlite` and `php_sqlite3` → Restart All
   Services.**
3. Visit **http://localhost/foodiie/install/** and complete the installer
   (creates the admin account).
4. Site: **http://localhost/foodiie/public/** — Admin:
   **http://localhost/foodiie/admin/**

Optional: serve `public/` as its own host with a VirtualHost
(`C:\wamp64\bin\apache\apache2.x\conf\extra\httpd-vhosts.conf`):

```apache
<VirtualHost *:80>
    ServerName foodiie.local
    DocumentRoot "C:/wamp64/www/foodiie/public"
</VirtualHost>
```

Then add `127.0.0.1 foodiie.local` to `C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts`,
restart WAMP, and browse to `http://foodiie.local`. Set
`APP_URL=http://foodiie.local` in `.env` if you do this.

## 1. Run locally (php -S)

From the project root:

```bash
cd foodiie
php -S localhost:8000
```

Then open:

- Installer: `http://localhost:8000/install/`
- Public site: `http://localhost:8000/public/`
- Admin: `http://localhost:8000/admin/`

Use `APP_URL=http://localhost:8000/public` in `.env` for local development.
(The built-in server ignores `.htaccess`; that is fine for local dev.)

## 2. Create the first admin (installer)

1. Open `/install/` in the browser.
2. The installer runs system checks (PHP ≥ 7.4, `pdo_sqlite`, `sqlite3`,
   `gd`, `mbstring`, `dom`, `zip`, writable folders). Fix any FAIL rows —
   on WAMP this is almost always a missing PHP extension (see above).
3. Fill in site name, admin name/email/password (min 8 chars), confirm
   the auto-detected public URL, tick/untick demo content, and click
   **Install FOODIIE**.
4. The installer creates the SQLite database, the admin user, seeds
   settings/categories/pages (+ demo content if ticked), writes
   `install.lock`, locks down `install/` via `.htaccess`, creates
   `.env` with your `APP_URL` (only if `.env` does not exist yet),
   and generates the static public site so it is live immediately.

The installer refuses to run twice while `install.lock` exists.

## 3. Create an article

1. Log in at `/admin/` → **Articles → New Article**.
2. Fill in title (slug auto-generates), category, description (used for
   meta description), body (rich text; only safe HTML is kept), tags,
   and an optional cover image (uploaded to `storage/uploads/`).
3. Fill **SEO title** and **SEO description** (used for `<title>` and
   meta tags on the generated page), or leave them to fall back to the
   title/description.
4. Click **Save Draft**. Nothing appears on the public site yet.

Recipes (**Recipes → New Recipe**) and videos (**Videos → New Video**)
work the same way: structured fields (ingredients, steps, times, YouTube
URL) instead of a free-form body.

## 4. Publish an article

1. Open the article in the admin and set **Status → Published**.
2. Set **Publish at** to now (or a future date/time to schedule it —
   see *Scheduled publishing* below).
3. Save, then regenerate the site (**Dashboard → Regenerate**, or
   `php cms/generator/generate.php`).

The article page appears in `public/` only after regeneration. To
unpublish, set status back to Draft and regenerate.

## 5. How static pages are generated

`cms/generator/generate.php` reads published rows from SQLite and writes
complete HTML files into `public/`:

- `/` homepage, `/recipes/`, `/videos/`, category archives
- `/article/<slug>/`, `/recipe/<slug>/`, `/video/<slug>/`
- static pages (`/about/`, `/contact/`, …), `sitemap.xml`, `feed.xml`

Run it manually after content changes:

```bash
php cms/generator/generate.php
```

Only rows with `status = published` and `publish_at <= now` are
generated. Drafts and future-dated posts stay out of `public/`.

**Scheduled publishing** — run the generator every minute so scheduled
posts go live on time:

```cron
* * * * * php /path/to/foodiie/cms/generator/generate.php --publish-due
```

(On Windows/WAMP use Task Scheduler → run
`C:\wamp64\bin\php\php7.4.x\php.exe C:\wamp64\www\foodiie\cms\generator\generate.php --publish-due`
every minute.)

## 6. Deploy the public site — Option A (static host + PHP host)

Best for speed and cost: the public site on a static host, the CMS on a
PHP host.

1. On your local machine or PHP host, run `php cms/generator/generate.php`.
2. Upload the **contents of `public/`** to Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, or
   GitHub Pages (drag-and-drop deploy works).
3. Host the **whole project** (admin, cms, storage) on a PHP 7.4+ host —
   e.g. `cms.foodiie.in` or a subdirectory the public never sees.
4. Set `APP_URL=https://foodiie.in` in the CMS host's `.env` and
   regenerate before each deploy so canonical/OG URLs are correct.
5. Workflow for new posts: write in admin → regenerate → re-upload
   `public/` (or automate with the cron + a deploy hook).

## 7. Deploy the CMS — Option B (everything on one PHP host)

Simplest on shared hosting:

1. Upload the whole project to the host.
2. Point the domain's document root at `public/` if the host allows it;
   otherwise upload as-is and add a root `.htaccess` redirect to
   `public/` (the included `.htaccess` already blocks `.env`,
   `install.lock`, and `*.sqlite` from being served).
3. Visit `https://yourdomain.com/install/` and run the installer.
4. Set `APP_URL=https://foodiie.in` in `.env`.
5. Add the cron from section 5 so scheduled posts publish automatically.

## 8. Connect the foodiie.in domain

1. **DNS:** in your registrar/DNS panel, add an `A` record for `@`
   pointing at your host's IP (and a `CNAME` for `www` → `foodiie.in`).
   For Option A, point `@`/`www` at the static host per its docs
   (usually a CNAME or their anycast IPs).
2. **APP_URL:** set `APP_URL=https://foodiie.in` in `.env`
   (no trailing slash), then regenerate the site so every canonical,
   sitemap, and feed URL uses the domain.
3. **HTTPS:** enable the host's free SSL (Let's Encrypt / Cloudflare)
   and confirm the site loads over `https://`.

## 9. Google Search Console setup

1. Go to [Search Console](https://search.google.com/search-console) →
   **Add property** → URL prefix → `https://foodiie.in`.
2. Verify ownership: copy the verification code and paste it into
   **Admin → SEO → Search Console verification** (or set
   `GOOGLE_SEARCH_CONSOLE_VERIFICATION=` in `.env`), regenerate, and
   click Verify.
3. In Search Console → **Sitemaps**, submit
   `https://foodiie.in/sitemap.xml` (generated automatically).
4. Optional: set `GOOGLE_ANALYTICS_ID=` in `.env` (or Admin → SEO) to
   enable analytics output.

## 10. Configure advertising

1. **Admin → Ads** lists the 7 slots: header, homepage, article_top,
   article_middle, article_bottom, sidebar, footer.
2. Paste your ad code (e.g. AdSense responsive unit) into a slot, set
   device targeting (desktop / mobile / both), optional start/end
   dates, and toggle **Enabled**.
3. For AdSense: put your publisher ID in `ADSENSE_PUBLISHER_ID=` in
   `.env` (or Admin → SEO) so the account meta/script is included, then
   paste each ad unit's code into the slots you want.
4. Regenerate the site. Disabled or empty slots render nothing visible.

## 11. Backups

**Admin → Backup** creates a downloadable archive of the SQLite
database plus `storage/uploads/`. Recommended routine:

- Download a backup **before every update** and monthly otherwise.
- Keep at least one off-site copy (email it to yourself, or copy to
  cloud storage).
- The database file also lives at `cms/data/foodiie.sqlite` — copying
  that file while the site is idle is a valid quick backup.

To restore: replace `cms/data/foodiie.sqlite` and `storage/uploads/`
with the backup copies, then regenerate.

## 12. Updating the system

1. **Back up** (Admin → Backup).
2. Replace all project files **except**: `.env`, `install.lock`,
   `cms/data/`, and `storage/` (your content and uploads live there).
3. If the release notes mention a database migration, run it
   (documented per release); otherwise no DB change is needed.
4. Regenerate the site and spot-check the homepage and one article.

## Security notes

- The installer locks itself (`install.lock` + `install/.htaccess`
  denies all access). You may delete `install/` entirely after setup.
- `.env`, `*.sqlite`, backups and logs are blocked from web access by
  the root `.htaccess`. Never commit `.env` to git.
- Admin passwords are hashed with `password_hash()`; admin routes
  require login (see `admin/includes/guard.php`).
- Article/page bodies are sanitized to a safe HTML allowlist on save.
- Keep PHP and WAMP/hosting packages updated; always serve the public
  site over HTTPS in production.

## .env reference

See `.env.example` for the full template. The keys that matter:

| Key | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `APP_URL` | Canonical public URL, no trailing slash (`https://foodiie.in`; WAMP: `http://localhost/foodiie/public`) |
| `BASE_PATH` | URL path prefix override; leave empty to derive from `APP_URL` |
| `APP_ENV` | `production` or `development` |
| `DATABASE_PATH` | Override SQLite path (default `cms/data/foodiie.sqlite`) |
| `UPLOAD_PATH` | Override uploads dir (default `storage/uploads`) |
| `GOOGLE_ANALYTICS_ID` | GA4 measurement ID (also in Admin → SEO) |
| `GOOGLE_SEARCH_CONSOLE_VERIFICATION` | GSC verification token (also in Admin → SEO) |
| `ADSENSE_PUBLISHER_ID` | AdSense `ca-pub-…` ID (also in Admin → SEO) |
| `META_PIXEL_ID` | Meta Pixel ID (also in Admin → SEO) |
| `MAIL_*` | SMTP settings for contact-form notifications |

